WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.071
you know from films
such as "Forrest Gump,"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.541
"Philadelphia," "Castaway,"
"Apollo 13," and so much more.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:12.011
He stars in "A Man Called Otto,"
which is in select theaters now

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.513
and is playing everywhere
this Friday.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:14.781
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.683
-So press in on the clutch
and give it a little gas.

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:19.786
Slowly let out on the --
oh, you're not in gear.

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.020
[ Horn honking ]

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.855
Look, don't --
you don't have to turn the key.

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:24.123
-Aah!
-Go!

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:25.758
-Just press --
-Oh, my God.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:26.759
[ Horn honking ]
-Press on --

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.093
-[ Speaking in Spanish ]

00:00:28.093 --> 00:00:30.463
-Slowly let out on the clutch
and press on the gas.

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.932
[ Horn honking ]
Now you listen to me.

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:35.001
You have given birth
to two children.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.402
You learned a new language.

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.671
You will have no problem
learning how to drive.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.907
My God, the world
is full of complete idiots

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.207
who have managed
to figure it out,

00:00:42.207 --> 00:00:44.210
and you are not
a complete idiot.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:51.550
So clutch, shift, gas, drive.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.554
-Please welcome back to the show
Tom Hanks, everybody!

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:56.222
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:56.222 --> 00:01:06.232 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:12.138 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:15.408
You're excited.
-I'm very excited by --

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.510
I'm very excited
by The Strokes, man.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.747
It's not often you -- how many
guitars are in The Strokes?

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:23.015
-Two guitars.
-Two guitars and the drums.

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.750
There you go.
That's all you need, baby.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.487
That's Cream, man.
That is Cream, as in the Eric --

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:29.855
You know.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.591
The old Cream, not the --
-I'm so excited about this,

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:33.760
that just for -- okay.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.194
You're gonna
have to make a decision.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:38.531
-Here is one lottery ticket
for the Powerball.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.634
I have one more.
Now, this is worth $2 billion.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.903
-Right.
-One of these will be yours.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.137
The other one
will go to the band.

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:46.239
-Okay.
-Which one do you want?

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.873
-Oh, interesting.
-Are you feeling lucky?

00:01:47.873 --> 00:01:49.508
-I'm gonna take this one.
-All right!

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:50.810
-And I'm gonna
give that one to the band.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.480
-All right.
-It would be such --

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.682
You'd think it'd be a burn
if they won,

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.517
but I would be so happy
'cause they'd have to split it.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:01:59.852
-Oh, that's right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:03.122
Now, the other thing --
They would have to split it.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.324
But I think The Strokes' drummer
should get just a little --

00:02:05.324 --> 00:02:06.959
-Yeah!
-Yeah.

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.061
I think a little bit more, yeah.
-Just a little bit.

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:10.263
-[ Laughs ]
[ Applause ]

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.598
But you do -- if you win,
you still gotta swear

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:14.433
you're coming to work
the next day, pal.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:15.735
-Yeah.
-You know what? We all gotta --

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.336
-Nobody gets to take it off
just 'cause they win

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:18.537
the Mega Millions.
-Not good.

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:20.039
-Hey, you know what I think
is really funny?

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:22.541
And I want to stress this is a
choice our show makes, not you.

00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:25.411
As I was introducing you
and I was saying things like,

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.880
"You know him from
'Forrest Gump'" and --

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.049
you know, that's helpful,
I think, for some actors,

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.485
but I don't think our audience
needed to hear those things.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.655
I don't think, if I hadn't
mentioned "Forrest Gump,"

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.156
Tom Hanks would walk out,
and they'd be like,

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:39.792
"What do I know him from?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:43.196
-All right.
You know, very kind of you.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.931
It's very nice.
Thank you.

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:47.200
Thank you.
I have to blame home video

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.336
for the familiarity because,
you know, between --

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:54.707
between VHS machines and DVDs,
man, you get to --

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.575
you all grew up with me
one way or another.

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:57.777
-Yeah.
-Am I wrong?

00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:59.712
"All right.
We're going out," and you know.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:02.048
-That was a good era.
The VHS era

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.217
was a good time to get started.
-Wasn't it?

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.784
Wasn't it fabulous?
-Yeah.

00:03:05.784 --> 00:03:08.054
It's crazy to me the idea of --

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.290
I just -- the amount of movies
I watched 15, 20, 30 times.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.092
It was so easy for me.
Like, when you were growing up,

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.728
were there movies that
you watched over and over?

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.429
-But you had --
they had to land, you know?

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:19.632
They had to be on channel 5
or something like that.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.267
And they were always
edited for commercials.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.069
I remember the first time I saw
"The Maltese Falcon,"

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.240
it was only like 47 minutes long
because they just cut out

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.875
huge storylines, you know?
-Yeah.

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:31.143
-There was, what,
Archer and Spade?

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:33.645
There was no Archer in it,
was just Sam Spade.

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:36.482
So you had to deal with that.
But I -- The -- The --

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:38.984
There was a time
when I predicted

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:42.154
at, like, New Year's,
I predicted that, in the future,

00:03:42.154 --> 00:03:45.358
we will all
just have a box on our TV,

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:48.461
and from that box,
we can watch anything we want.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:51.230
We can watch documentaries
from 1952.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.366
We can watch Edward R. --
We can all watch old episodes

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:56.135
of "The Dick Van --"
watch any movie that we want to.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.638
And I thought it would be there.
I just didn't realize

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:00.105
it would each --
it was gonna cost us

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:02.174
10 bucks a month for five
different subscription services.

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:03.309
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:04.744
-I thought it was,
"Well, you pay a buck

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.579
for each thing,
and that works out."

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.081
I didn't realize you would
have to have passcodes,

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:11.050
an accountant
that you had to pay for.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.152
-Yeah.
-You know, nine different

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:16.188
remotes of menu, outputs,
you know.

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:18.491
-Do you ever have that --
I have this point with my kids,

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.992
who are obviously
a great deal younger than yours,

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.894
where they'll
get really excited.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:22.928
I'll say,
"We're gonna show you a movie,"

00:04:22.928 --> 00:04:24.463
and maybe, let's say,
it's on Disney+.

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:25.598
And we'll sit down,
I'll turn it on,

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.233
and it'll need
the password again,

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:28.467
and I will just say to them,

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:29.668
"It doesn't have
the movie you want."

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:31.737
-"We're not gonna have it."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:35.541
-"Disney+ is broken today."
-We can't --

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:37.243
"They decided
that you are not allowed

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.278
to watch this movie tonight."
-Yeah.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.481
-"So let's play 'Clue.'"
-Yep, and there we go.

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.382
-Pull out the "Clue,"
and it's Colonel Mustard

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:45.951
in the study with the lead pipe.
-So you --

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:47.520
There's a wonderful scene
where you're teaching someone

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.456
to drive in this film.
Now, again, your kids are older.

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.991
You've gone through this.
Were you good --

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.026
Did you actually teach
your children how to drive,

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:55.694
and were you patient?
-Well, okay.

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.763
You've got youngsters,
so you put them on the lap.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:04:59.264
You go to a big vacant lot.
-Uh-huh.

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:01.900
-You let them steer around,
and you control the pedals.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.003
-Okay.
-Or, you know, you're coming up,

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.339
somebody's got a -- "Hey, it's a
quarter mile from here to there.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:08.674
Sit behind, and I'll shift
for you and all that."

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:12.111
Then they get into where
they are sitting actually

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.813
in the driver's seat,
and you're riding shotgun,

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:16.115
and all you say is,
"What are you doing?

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.283
What are you doing?
What are you doing?"

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:18.417
-Yeah.
-"What are you doing?

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.921
No, no, no, no.
Easy, easy, easy. Easy!

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:24.457
Turn left, left, left!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:28.260
"I'm absolutely --
No, turn that down."

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.896
That's the other thing.
"Turn it down!

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:32.565
Turn it down!"
-See, I'm glad to hear

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:35.535
you're like this because I feel
that is the future for me

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:38.337
just based on I'm trying
to teach them how to ski,

00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:40.206
and the instruction
that I usually give

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:42.408
is something along
the lines of "Just ski!"

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.709
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:46.545
"Go down. Go down.
-Look, do what everyone else

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:48.347
here is doing.
-Pole, plant, pole, plant.

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.215
But, look, in all reality,

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:53.785
here's how we're gonna teach
our kids how to drive.

00:05:53.785 --> 00:05:55.755
Sit down.
Enter your destination.

00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:57.155
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.155 --> 00:05:59.826
Take a nap, and that's --
and they will get there

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:02.394
at some point.
-You -- a very cool thing

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:05.197
in this film,
you play Otto in present day.

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:07.500
Your son Truman...
-Yes, my son Truman.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.102
-...your actual son Truman
plays you in the flashbacks,

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:12.538
and this is,
if not his first role,

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.942
certainly his first major role.
Were you excited for him?

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:18.578
-I was --
It's an intimidating process.

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:21.481
Marc Forster, who directed it,
he said, "Why do we have to have

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:23.449
a look-alike for you
to play the young" --

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.918
He's from Switzerland,
all right?

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:28.120
-Yeah, I got it.
I think we were worried.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.790
We're like --
-He was not interrogating us.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.592
He was actually suggesting
a better way

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.059
in order to make the movie.

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:35.794
"Can't one of your kids
come on and play you?

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:37.797
I think Truman
looks very much like you."

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.565
I said, "Well,
you'll have to talk to him."

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:43.068
So, he actually is a member
of the -- proud member of

00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:46.038
Local 600 of the New York City
camera union.

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.840
So he actually --
he's actually --

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.311
he's actually loading film
on episodic TV today.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:54.313
-Oh, my God.
-He's a renaissance dude.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.215
And I said,
"Look, if you want to do this,

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:58.918
you know, understand
you got to fight

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:00.519
self-consciousness like crazy.

00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:02.020
There's only one way to do it.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.925
Show up, know your stuff,
let go, and ski!

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:07.293
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:08.361
-There you go.
"Why can't you ski?!"

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:09.895
-"Why can't you just ski?!"

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.964
And he did it okay.
-I thought he was great.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.298
I have a lot more to ask you.

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:15.701 line:0%
We'll be right back with more
from the great Tom Hanks.

